# RedLab Cursor AI Rules

# Context & Product
- Project: RedLab (Visual Cloud Security Lab MVP)
- Stack: React 19 + TypeScript + Vite + Zustand + React Flow (Frontend) | FastAPI + SQLAlchemy + SQLite (Backend)
- Theme: Dark Cybersecurity Laboratory (#0B0D12, #11151D, #171C26, #293140, #FF3B4E). Never use bright/white themes.

# Strict Architecture Guidelines
1. State: Use Zustand (`useEditorStore` and `useLabStore`). Never put shared editor state in local component state.
2. Canvas: Visual nodes/edges must adhere to `@xyflow/react` conventions and serialize cleanly to `GraphData` (nodes/edges).
3. Security Rules: Must be deterministic Python classes subclassing `SecurityRule` in `backend/app/security_engine/registry.py`. Never hardcode mock findings in endpoints or frontend.
4. Schemas: Keep `backend/app/schemas/lab.py` and `frontend/src/types/index.ts` synchronized.
5. No External Cloud: Purely simulated. Do NOT call real AWS APIs or provision real cloud infrastructure.

# Git Branching
- Never commit to `main` or `develop`.
- Work only in designated feature branches:
  - `feature/canvas-*` (Canvas & React Flow)
  - `feature/ui-*` (Inspector forms, modals, dashboard)
  - `feature/security-*` (Security engine rules, attack paths)
  - `feature/backend-*` (FastAPI endpoints, DB, import/export)
  - `feature/qa-*` (Tests & CI)
- Follow conventional commits: `feat:`, `fix:`, `test:`, `refactor:`, `docs:`.

# Testing Mandate
- Frontend: Run `npm run build` and `npm test` inside `frontend/` before declaring work done.
- Backend: Run `python3 -m pytest tests/ -v` inside `backend/` before declaring work done.
